NAME
       od - dump a file in octal, hexadecimal or as characters

SYNOPSIS
       od [-c] [-x] [-t TYPE] [-A RADIX] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       od prints the bytes of a file so that invisible characters can be
       seen. Each line starts with the offset (in octal by default) followed
       by the data. Without options it shows the data as octal numbers, two
       bytes at a time. It is handy for finding stray tabs, carriage returns
       (\r, from files made on Windows) or a missing final newline.

OPTIONS
       -c     Show printable characters as themselves and the others as
              escapes such as \n, \t, \r or octal numbers.

       -x     Show the data as hexadecimal numbers, two bytes at a time.

       -t TYPE
              Choose the format: c characters, x1 hexadecimal bytes, o1
              octal bytes, d1 decimal bytes.

       -A RADIX
              How the offsets are written: o octal, d decimal, x hex, n none.

EXAMPLES
           od -c /etc/hostname
           printf 'a\tb\r\n' | od -c

SEE ALSO
       hexdump(1), xxd(1), cat(1)
